ERR1 = "Add a delay in this loop since a conditional entry call or selective accept with an else can otherwise busy-wait"


def ids():
    return ("ADA95_6.2.5",)


def name(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.2.5": "Published Standards/Ada 95/6.2.5 Selective Accepts and Entry Calls",
    }[id]


def tags(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.2.5": [
            "Language: Ada",
            "Standard: Ada 95",
            "Concurrency",
        ],
    }.get(id, [])


def detailed_description(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.2.5": """
<p><b>Guideline</b></p>
<p>&#8226; Use caution with conditional entry calls to task entries.</p>
<p>&#8226; Use caution with selective accepts with else parts.</p>
<p>&#8226; Do not depend upon a particular delay in timed entry calls to
task entries.</p>
<p>&#8226; Do not depend upon a particular delay in selective accepts with
delay alternatives.</p>
<p>&#8226; Consider using protected objects instead of the rendezvous for
data-oriented synchronization.</p>

<p><b>Example</b></p>
<p>The conditional entry call in the following code results in a
potential race condition that might degenerate into a busy waiting loop
(i.e., perform the same calculation over and over). The task
Current_Position containing entry Request_New_Coordinates might never
execute if the loop-containing task (shown in the following code
fragment) has a higher priority than Current_Position because it does
not release the processing resource:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">task body Calculate_Flightpath is
begin
   ...
   loop

         select
            Current_Position.Request_New_Coordinates (X, Y);
            -- calculate projected location based on new coordinates
            ...

         else
            -- calculate projected location based on last locations
            ...
         end select;
   end loop;
   ...
end Calculate_Flightpath;
</code></pre>
<p>The addition of a delay, as shown, may allow Current_Position to
execute until it reaches an accept for Request_New_Coordinates:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">task body Calculate_Flightpath is
begin
   ...
   loop

         select
            Current_Position.Request_New_Coordinates(X, Y);
            -- calculate projected location based on new coordinates
            ...
         else
            -- calculate projected location based on last locations
            ...

            delay until Time_To_Execute;
            Time_To_Execute := Time_To_Execute + Period;
         end select;
   end loop;
   ...
end Calculate_Flightpath;
</code></pre>
<p>The following selective accept with else again does not degenerate
into a busy wait loop only because of the addition of a delay statement:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">task body Buffer_Messages is
   ...

begin
   ...

   loop
      delay until Time_To_Execute;

         select
            accept Get_New_Message (Message : in       String) do
                -- copy message to parameters
                ...
            end Get_New_Message;
         else -- Don't wait for rendezvous
            -- perform built in test Functions
            ...
         end select;

      Time_To_Execute := Time_To_Execute + Period;
   end loop;

   ...

end Buffer_Messages;
</code></pre>
<p>The following timed entry call might be considered an unacceptable
implementation if lost communications with the reactor for over 25
milliseconds results in a critical situation:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">task body Monitor_Reactor is
   ...
begin
   ...
   loop

      select
         Reactor.Status(OK);

      or
         delay 0.025;
         -- lost communication for more that 25 milliseconds
         Emergency_Shutdown;
      end select;
       -- process reactor status
       ...
   end loop;
   ...
end Monitor_Reactor;
</code></pre>
<p>In the following &ldquo;selective accept with delay&rdquo; example, the
accuracy of the coordinate calculation function is bounded by time. For
example, the required accuracy cannot be obtained unless Period is
within + or &#8211; 0.005 seconds. This period cannot be guaranteed
because of the inaccuracy of the delay statement:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">task body Current_Position is
begin
   ...
   loop

      select
         accept Request_New_Coordinates (X :          out Integer;
                                          Y :         out Integer) do
             -- copy coordinates to parameters
             ...
         end Request_New_Coordinates;
      or
         delay until Time_To_Execute;
      end select;

       Time_To_Execute := Time_To_Execute + Period;
       -- Read Sensors
       -- execute coordinate transformations
   end loop;
   ...
end Current_Position;
</code></pre>

<p><b>Rationale</b></p>
<p>Use of these constructs always poses a risk of race conditions. Using
them in loops, particularly with poorly chosen task priorities, can have
the effect of busy waiting.</p>
<p>These constructs are very much implementation dependent. For
conditional entry calls and selective accepts with else parts, the Ada
Reference Manual (1995, &sect;9.7) does not define &ldquo;immediately.&rdquo;
For timed entry calls and selective accepts with delay alternatives,
implementors might have ideas of time that differ from each other and
from your own. Like the delay statement, the delay alternative on the
select construct might wait longer than the time required (see
Guideline 6.1.7).</p>
<p>Protected objects offer an efficient means for providing data-oriented
synchronization. Operations on protected objects incur less execution
overhead than tasks and are more efficient for data synchronization and
communication than the rendezvous. See Guideline 6.1.1 for an example of
this use of protected objects.</p>

<p><b>Developer's Note</b></p>
<p>The first two bullets are automated together as one check, following
the guideline's own examples directly: a <code>select</code> statement with an
<code>else</code> part (whether its first alternative is an entry call or an
<code>accept</code> &#8212; the risk and the remedy are the same either way) is flagged
when it sits inside a loop that contains no <code>delay</code> or <code>delay until</code>
statement anywhere in its body, matching exactly the difference between
the guideline's first (bad) and second (fixed by adding a delay)
Calculate_Flightpath examples. This is a structural proxy, not a proof of
actual busy-waiting: a <code>delay</code> anywhere in the loop is accepted even if
it sits on a rarely taken path that would not actually pace the common
case, and a loop with no <code>delay</code> is still flagged even if its <code>else</code>
branch happens to do enough real work of its own (blocking I/O, for
example) to avoid spinning in practice. A <code>select</code> with an <code>else</code> that
is not inside any loop at all is not flagged, since a one-shot
conditional entry call cannot busy-wait.</p>
<p>The remaining three bullets are not automated. "Do not depend upon a
particular delay" (in either the timed-entry-call or selective-accept-
with-delay form) is the same caution as <code>ADA95_6.1.7</code>'s "do not depend on
a particular delay being achievable," with no structural signal beyond
the mere presence of a delay, which is not itself a defect. "Consider
using protected objects instead of the rendezvous" is a design-choice
recommendation with no violation shape, the same as <code>ADA95_6.1.1</code> and
<code>ADA95_6.2.1</code>.</p>
""",
    }[id]


def test_entity(file):
    return file.kind().check("Ada File")


def test_language(language):
    return language == "Ada"


_OPENER_KEYWORDS = ("if", "case", "loop", "select", "begin", "do")


def _next_significant(lex):
    lex = lex.next()

    while lex and lex.token() in ("Whitespace", "Newline", "Comment"):
        lex = lex.next()

    return lex


def _is_statement_start(lex):
    prev = lex.previous(True, True)

    return prev is None or prev.text() in (";", "then", "else", "loop", "begin", "do")


def _loop_ranges(lexer):
    # A single stack keyed only on "loop"/"end loop" correctly tracks loop
    # nesting on its own, since Ada requires every construct to be
    # properly nested regardless of what else (if/case/select/begin/do)
    # is interleaved between a loop's own start and its own end.
    ranges = []
    stack = []
    lex = lexer.first()

    while lex:
        if lex.token() == "Keyword" and lex.text().lower() == "loop":
            prev = lex.previous(True, True)

            if prev is None or prev.text().lower() != "end":
                stack.append(lex.line_begin())
        elif lex.token() == "Keyword" and lex.text().lower() == "end":
            nxt = _next_significant(lex)

            if nxt and nxt.text().lower() == "loop" and stack:
                ranges.append((stack.pop(), lex.line_begin()))

        lex = lex.next()

    return ranges


def _innermost_range(line, ranges):
    home = None

    for start, end in ranges:
        if start <= line <= end:
            if home is None or (end - start) < (home[1] - home[0]):
                home = (start, end)

    return home


def _select_has_else(lex):
    # lex is positioned right after the statement's own "select" keyword.
    # See ADA95_6.2.2/ADA95_6.2.4 for the depth-counting rationale: a
    # single counter over if/case/loop/select/begin/do, closed by any
    # "end", is enough to skip nested constructs and find this select's
    # own top-level "else"/"end select".
    depth = 0
    has_else = False

    while lex:
        text = lex.text().lower() if lex.token() == "Keyword" else None

        if text in _OPENER_KEYWORDS:
            depth += 1
            lex = _next_significant(lex)
            continue

        if text == "end":
            if depth == 0:
                return has_else

            depth -= 1
            lex = _next_significant(lex)
            continue

        if depth == 0 and text == "else":
            has_else = True

        lex = _next_significant(lex)

    return has_else


def _find_selects_with_else(lexer):
    selects = []
    lex = lexer.first()

    while lex:
        if (
            lex.token() == "Keyword"
            and lex.text().lower() == "select"
            and _is_statement_start(lex)
        ):
            select_line, select_col = lex.line_begin(), lex.column_begin()

            if _select_has_else(_next_significant(lex)):
                selects.append((select_line, select_col))

        lex = lex.next()

    return selects


def _has_delay(lexer, start_line, end_line):
    lex = lexer.lexeme(start_line, 1)

    while lex and lex.line_begin() <= end_line:
        if (
            lex.token() == "Keyword"
            and lex.text().lower() == "delay"
            and _is_statement_start(lex)
        ):
            return True

        lex = lex.next()

    return False


def check(check, file):
    lexer = file.lexer(lookup_ents=False)
    loop_ranges = _loop_ranges(lexer)

    for select_line, select_col in _find_selects_with_else(lexer):
        enclosing_loop = _innermost_range(select_line, loop_ranges)

        if enclosing_loop is None:
            continue

        start, end = enclosing_loop

        if not _has_delay(lexer, start, end):
            check.violation(None, file, select_line, select_col, ERR1)
